  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1125 AM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nPatchy clouds are beginning to develop today. Temperatures have\nreached the lower 80s for most sites, en route to highs in the\nupper 80s to near 90. Dew points are in the upper 60s to near\n70, keeping humidity levels bearable for the time being. Dry\nweather is expected today as high pressure moves through the\nOhio River Valley.\n\nRidging is present in the western third of the country, with a \nshortwave ridge to our east. In between, a shortwave trough over\nthe Dakotas today will slowly drift southeastward, crossing the\nQuad State on Saturday. A fairly strong moisture plume will \nbring precipitable water values up to 2 inches in SEMO by late \nFriday afternoon. Showers and storms are possible during the \nmidday to afternoon hours Friday, entering from the west. \nBetter forcing arrives Friday evening as the low pressure center\napproaches and warm/cold fronts approach the CWA. MLCAPE of \n2000J/kg and deep layer shear of 25-35kts in Southeast Missouri\nmaintain a D2 Marginal Risk for that area, while both CAPE and \nshear values drop off further east.\n\nAs the low pressure system drifts from Illinois to Indiana, a\ndry slot intrudes into the Quad State from the southwest.\nDepending on the forward (eastward) speed of the low,\nredevelopment Saturday afternoon can produce some stronger\nstorms, mainly in the Kentucky Pennyrile area. From a rainfall\nperspective, a slight risk ERO covers mainly Southern Illinois \nfor Friday, but QPF has lowered to a quarter to three-quarters\nof an inch (NBM 1\" probabilities of 20-40% from south to north)\nso overperforming would be necessary to result in any flooding.\n\nSunday into early next week, models are in strong agreement on\nthe low pressure system getting cut out of the trough, but\ndiffer on how it drifts afterwards. The operational GFS sticks\nfurther northeast while the Euro keeps it in the mid-South and\nCanadian sends it southeastward. Ensembles, however, suggest\nthat the GEFS might be the furthest west in positioning of the\ntrough axis. Daily, primarily diurnally driven, PoPs remain\nSunday into early next week due to the proximity of the cutoff\nlow. Eastern portions of the Quad State appear to be most\nfavored for showers/storms. Once the system moves away from\nareas, very dry mid-level air moves in behind it.\n\nSlightly below normal temperatures today find slight warming\n(mainly with low temperatures) as moisture increases Friday. A \ncold frontal passage Saturday night cools the region for Sunday \ninto early next week, with temperatures rising to the 90s \nmidweek as drier conditions move in.",
